  "body": "## What to build\n\nStore shared components on the document and add undoable commands so changes to one component update every assembly that uses it.\n\n1. Declare document component storage and per-tool assembly link components in `crates/app/src/schema.rs` (following the schema layering rule).\n2. Add undoable commands in `crates/app/src/commands/`:\n   - `UpsertDocumentComponent`: adds a new validated component to the document table.\n   - `EditDocumentComponent`: updates an existing document component, re-validates every tool assembly that references it, and updates each referencing tool's derived `ToolDef` and `ToolBody` in one atomic undo step.\n   - `DeleteDocumentComponent`: deletes an unused document component; refuses by name if any tool assembly still references it.\n   - `SetToolAssembly`: creates or replaces a tool's component stack, checks compatibility and geometry fit at construction, and lands the derived `ToolDef` and `ToolBody` in one undo step.\n3. Let existing `GenStamp` and `tool_body::digest` staleness derivation mark affected operations `Stale` automatically when a shared component edit moves cutting parameters or collision bands.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Depends on the component model and geometry resolver sub-issues of #2452.\n- Never allow a breaking component edit to leave an invalid or mismatched assembly in the document.\n\n## Done means\n\n- Tests in `crates/app/tests/` prove that editing one shared turning insert (for example changing `nose_radius` or cutting data) updates two different holder assemblies in one undo step and marks operations using both tools `Stale`.\n- Tests prove that editing a shared component in a way that breaks compatibility on any referencing assembly is refused by name and changes nothing.\n- Tests prove that deleting a referenced component is refused naming the referencing tools.\n- Tests prove `undo` and `redo` restore the exact prior component and all referencing tools and bodies.\n\nPart of #2452\n",
